  "language": "en",
  "text": "Operation Silent Current: synthetic test report (not real threat intelligence)\n\nThe intrusion began when the operators sent spearphishing emails with a malicious Microsoft Word attachment to staff in the finance department. When a user opened the document and enabled macros, the macro launched PowerShell with an encoded command.\n\nThe PowerShell script downloaded a second-stage loader from a compromised WordPress site and saved it in the user's AppData folder. To survive reboots, the loader created a scheduled task named OneDriveUpdate that ran every hour. It also added a Run key to the registry.\n\nOnce on the host, the actors ran whoami, ipconfig and net group \"domain admins\" /domain to learn about the user and the network. They used a renamed copy of Mimikatz to dump credentials from LSASS memory.\n\nWith the stolen administrator password, the operators moved laterally to the file server over RDP and used PsExec to run commands on several workstations. The malware disabled Windows Defender real-time protection and cleared the Security event log to hide its activity.\n\nFiles from the finance share were collected, compressed into a password-protected RAR archive and staged in a temporary folder. The archive was exfiltrated over HTTPS to a cloud storage account controlled by the attackers. The backdoor communicated with its command and control server over HTTPS using a custom user agent.\n\nIndicators of compromise:\n\n44d88612fea8a8f36de82e1278abb02f 185.220.101.4 hxxps://update-cdn[.]com/load.bin\n\nIn the final stage the actors encrypted files on the file server and left a ransom note demanding payment in Bitcoin.",
  "declared_ttps": []
 },
